[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m ### Step 1: Use the given condition $ \overline{IA} \perp \overline{OI} $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m This is a key geometric condition. Let’s denote:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ I $ as the incenter,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ O $ as the circumcenter,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ IA \perp OI $.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m This implies that the line segment $ IA $ is perpendicular to the line segment $ OI $, and both are connected to the incenter and circumcenter of the triangle.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m We can use vector geometry or coordinate geometry to analyze this, but we can also use known properties of triangle centers.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m ---
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m ### Step 2: Use known formulas for inradius and circumradius
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m We are given:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ R = 13 $ (circumradius),
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ r = 6 $ (inradius),
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - We are to find $ AB \cdot AC $.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m Let’s denote:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ AB = c $,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ AC = b $,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - $ BC = a $,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m - Then $ AB \cdot AC = bc $.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25105)[0m 
